  "question": {
    "text": "In comparison to the zeolite process for the removal of permanent hardness, the synthetic resin&nbsp;method is"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "less efficient as it exchanges only anions.",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "more efficient as it can exchange both cations as well as anions.",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "less efficient as the resins cannot be regenerated.",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "more efficient as it can exchange only cations.",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "B"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Zeolite removes only cations from hard water, as given below by reaction:</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>NaZ</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>(</mo><mi>aq</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>&#8594;</mo><msub><mi>MZ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>Na</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mi>Here</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>Ca</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Mg</mi></math></p><p>While, synthetic resin removes both cations and anions, as given by following reactions:</p><p>For cations-</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>RNa</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>(</mo><mi>aq</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>&#8594;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>Na</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>(</mo><mi>aq</mi><mo>)</mo></math></p><p>For anions-</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi>RNH</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><msup><mi>OH</mi><mo>-</mo></msup><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mo>-</mo></msup><mo>(</mo><mi>aq</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#8594;</mo><msubsup><mi>RNH</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mo>-</mo></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>OH</mi><mo>-</mo></msup><mo>(</mo><mi>aq</mi><mo>)</mo></math></p><p>So, synthetic resin method is more efficient as it can exchange both cations as well anions.</p>"
  }
